---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="PMO Lead and automation developer at a professional services firm, working where operations, reporting, and technology meet."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>
        I build the infrastructure that makes a professional services firm run.
      </h1>

      <div class="about__intro">
        <figure class="about__portrait">
          <img
            src="/images/marisol.jpg"
            alt="Marisol Delbrey"
            width="1024"
            height="1280"
          />
        </figure>

        <div class="prose stack">
          <p>
            I studied philosophy because I wanted to understand how systems of
            reasoning actually work, the logic underneath decisions, arguments,
            and institutions. It turned out that same instinct applies just as
            well to how organizations run.
          </p>

          <p>
            I paired that with a CS minor at Chapman University, then spent a
            year as an ML/AI Fellow through a competitive UCLA and Cornell
            program, selected from over 1,500 applicants. I built applied
            machine learning skills there, working with Pandas, Scikit-Learn,
            and NumPy, and learned how to translate complex model outputs into
            something a non-technical stakeholder could actually use and trust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before Talley, I worked across a few different lenses on the same
            underlying question: how do you make complex systems legible and
            accountable? At Kaiser Permanente, that meant identifying
            operational inefficiencies in healthcare data workflows. At
            Accenture, it meant developing a data-backed strategy that placed in
            the top 0.05% of applicants to their Innovation Challenge. As a
            research assistant at Chapman's Fowler School of Engineering, it
            meant modeling California's water budget data to visualize decades
            of supply and demand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now, as PMO Lead at Talley LLP, I bring all of that together.
            Reporting directly to the COO, I build the reporting infrastructure,
            CRM systems, and automation that a professional services firm
            actually runs on, and I direct associate-level support to help
            scale that delivery. The work is different in scale from anything I
            did before, but the underlying question hasn't changed: how do you
            build something people can trust and actually use.
          </p>

          <p>Outside of work, I'm based in Orange County, California.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section about-details">
    <div class="wrap grid grid--2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="eyebrow">Focus areas</h2>
        <ul class="plain-list">
          <li>Project Management Office</li>
          <li>Workflow Automation</li>
          <li>AI Implementation</li>
          <li>Data Reporting and Operations</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="eyebrow">Background</h2>
        <ul class="plain-list">
          <li>PMO Lead, Professional Services Firm, 2025 to present</li>
          <li>Strategy and Technology Intern, Accenture</li>
          <li>
            PMP Candidate (exam target September 2026); Machine Learning
            Certification, Cornell University
          </li>
          <li>B.A. Philosophy, Computer Science Minor, Chapman University</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section about-cta">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="lead">
        I'm always open to conversations about operations, automation, and AI in
        professional services.
      </p>
      <a class="button button--accent" href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Portrait sits under the heading on phones and beside the copy on desktop.
     It is first in the DOM so it stays next to the heading when stacked. */
  .about__intro {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .about__intro {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 0.6fr;
    }

    .about__portrait {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1;
    }
  }

  .about__portrait {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Landscape source cropped to portrait; matches the hero framing. */
  .about__portrait img {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 47% 35%;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  .about-details,
  .about-cta {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .plain-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .plain-list li {
    padding: 0.6rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .about-cta .lead {
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }
</style>
